---
import ExperienceLayout from "../../layouts/ExperienceLayout.astro";
import { experiencePaths } from "../../features/experience/routes";
import NewsHeader from "../../features/experience/world/NewsHeader.astro";
import NewsFooter from "../../features/experience/world/NewsFooter.astro";
import "../../features/experience/world/news.css";
import {
  getSceneImages,
  sceneById
} from "../../features/experience/scenes.server";

const paths = experiencePaths(import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
const scenes = await getSceneImages();
const hall = sceneById(scenes, "hall");
---

<ExperienceLayout
  title="BROOD’s continuity archive — The Civic Margin"
  description="A fictional investigation into BROOD Civic Systems, part of an interactive surveillance satire."
  world="news"
>
  <div class="sheet">
    <NewsHeader active="investigation" />
    <main id="main-content">
      <article>
        <header class="article-header">
          <p class="rubric">
            <span class="fiction-tag"
              >Fictional investigation / BROOD story</span
            ><span>Public systems · information power</span>
          </p>
          <h1>
            BROOD calls an exposed observation archive a continuity feature.
          </h1>
          <p class="deck">
            An invented company promises that no one will be overlooked. Its
            fictional records suggest that being seen and agreeing to be watched
            were never the same requirement.
          </p>
          <div class="byline">
            <p>By the Civic Margin story desk</p><p>
              Interactive fiction · edition dated <time datetime="2026-10-31"
                >31 October 2026</time
              >
            </p>
          </div>
        </header>
        <div class="article-layout">
          <div class="article-body">
            <div class="reality-note">
              <p>
                BROOD, this publication, the review panel, and the events below
                are fictional. This story is an original satire. The real-world
                sources in the sidebar support the separate background
                discussion, not allegations against this invented company.
              </p>
            </div>
            <p>
              The first thing visible in the archive was an empty chair. Beyond
              it sat a terminal, a desk lamp, and a door left open into a
              service corridor. The room appeared prepared for someone. Its
              camera had already begun keeping the record.
            </p>
            <p>
              BROOD Civic Systems faces questions from a municipal oversight
              panel after a retained observation archive becomes accessible
              through its public materials. The company describes the exposure
              as a demonstration of operational continuity. The panel describes
              a different problem: material gathered for one purpose had become
              available for another, without the people concerned being asked.
            </p>
            <p>
              Participation, according to the company’s public materials,
              requires “no sign-up, no download.” BROOD advertises this as a
              convenience. When the panel asked how a resident could opt out,
              the company supplied instructions for opting a department in.
            </p>
            <p>
              The archive does not reveal a spectacular secret. It reveals a
              structure. Room views sit beside visit intervals, device reports,
              recurring selections, and the moments a visitor considers leaving.
              Individually, each entry looks administrative. Together, they
              become a story the subject did not write.
            </p>
            <h2>The company’s answer was that the pieces already existed.</h2>
            <p>
              BROOD’s acquisition process draws from supplied records,
              observation services, and information encountered online. The
              review panel questions whether those inputs were gathered and
              repurposed with permission. BROOD answers the permission question
              with an availability argument: if information can be reached, it
              can be made useful.
            </p>
            <blockquote class="pullquote">
              <p>“We do not create exposure. We make it legible.”</p><cite
                >Invented BROOD statement, within this fiction</cite
              >
            </blockquote>
            <p>
              That phrasing gives the company a convenient role. Exposure
              happens elsewhere; BROOD merely organizes it. Its public materials
              call the resulting picture care. A waiting room that remains ready
              becomes evidence of welcome. A road that never leaves the camera
              network becomes evidence of coverage. An empty control room
              becomes evidence that the service can continue without a witness.
            </p>
            <p>
              The panel’s concern is not simply whether a single record was
              public. It asks who joined the records, who decided how long to
              keep them, who gained access, and what the people represented
              could actually refuse. The company’s answer points back to its own
              service terms.
            </p>
            <h2>Visibility has a direction.</h2>
            <p>
              The archive offers a narrow view into the machinery of
              observation. The company regards this access as a courtesy,
              granted on its own terms. Permission to inspect the institution is
              presented as something different from permission to document it.
            </p>
            <p>
              “Publication remains a privilege,” another company statement
              reads. The distinction is difficult to miss. BROOD treats the
              public’s visibility as a useful condition. It treats its own
              visibility as an exception it has generously allowed.
            </p>
            <p>
              Its <a href={paths.confidence}>Public Confidence response</a> offers
              reassurance, a service register, and the promise that access remains
              managed. It does not answer the central question of the inquiry: who
              gets to decide when somebody else’s ordinary life becomes an institutional
              record?
            </p>
            <p>
              The chair in the archive remains empty. The system continues to
              report that everyone is accounted for.
            </p>
          </div>
          <aside class="article-aside" aria-label="Context and related reading">
            <section class="aside-block">
              <figure>
                <img
                  src={hall.src}
                  srcset={hall.srcSet}
                  width={hall.width}
                  height={hall.height}
                  sizes="(max-width: 550px) calc(100vw - 2rem), (max-width: 900px) 45vw, 300px"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                  alt="An empty office hallway with closed doors and framed pictures, one hanging slightly crooked."
                /><figcaption>
                  Company service corridor / fictional setting. Original scene
                  study.
                </figcaption>
              </figure><h2>Within the story</h2><p>
                The company, alleged collection practices, exposed archive, and
                municipal review are invented. No real person or company is the
                subject of these fictional allegations.
              </p><a href={paths.confidence}>Read BROOD’s response →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="aside-block">
              <h2>Outside the fiction</h2><p>
                In a March 2024 discussion, FTC staff described agency
                allegations concerning sensitive information handled by real
                data businesses, including X-Mode and InMarket. That documented
                background is separate from BROOD’s invented story.
              </p><a
                href="https://www.ftc.gov/policy/advocacy-research/tech-at-ftc/2024/03/ftc-cracks-down-mass-data-collectors-closer-look-avast-x-mode-inmarket"
                >Read the FTC discussion ↗</a
              >
            </section>
            <section class="aside-block">
              <h2>Related explainer</h2><h3 class="related-title">
                Public does not mean permission.
              </h3><p>
                What browser readings, commercial data markets, and privacy
                frameworks actually tell us—and what they do not.
              </p><a href={paths.explainer}>Read the sourced background →</a>
            </section>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </article>
    </main>
    <NewsFooter active="investigation" />
  </div>
</ExperienceLayout>
